WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.515
GPU spend is not only GPU time.

00:00:02.515 --> 00:00:06.655
Data location, storage type, dataset size, checkpoint strategy, logs, caches, temporary files,

00:00:06.655 --> 00:00:09.071
snapshots, and transfer patterns all affect cost.

00:00:09.071 --> 00:00:14.246
Poor data locality can make an expensive accelerator wait while data moves from somewhere else.

00:00:14.246 --> 00:00:17.006
Repeated dataset transfers are a common hidden cost.

00:00:17.006 --> 00:00:21.146
If every run copies the same large dataset to a new location,

00:00:21.146 --> 00:00:25.632
the team may pay in both transfer cost and lost productive GPU time.

00:00:25.632 --> 00:00:29.772
Better placement, staging, or caching can help when it is designed intentionally.

00:00:29.772 --> 00:00:33.222
Checkpoints and artifacts are valuable, but they need retention rules.

00:00:33.222 --> 00:00:35.982
During active training, checkpoints support recovery and comparison.

00:00:35.982 --> 00:00:40.812
After a decision is made, the team should know which files must be kept,

00:00:40.812 --> 00:00:43.573
which can be compacted, and which should expire.

00:00:43.573 --> 00:00:47.023
Without rules, old experiments can turn into long-term storage cost.

00:00:47.023 --> 00:00:50.128
Caches can save money when they reduce repeated work.

00:00:50.128 --> 00:00:54.613
They can also become waste if nobody owns them or measures their value.

00:00:54.613 --> 00:00:57.373
A useful cache has a purpose, a lifetime,

00:00:57.373 --> 00:01:00.134
and monitoring that shows whether it still helps.

00:01:00.134 --> 00:01:02.549
Logs and metrics need the same discipline.

00:01:02.549 --> 00:01:04.619
They should support troubleshooting, cost review,

00:01:04.619 --> 00:01:07.379
and operational accountability without becoming uncontrolled storage growth.

00:01:07.379 --> 00:01:12.899
Hidden costs become manageable when data movement and retention are treated as part of workload design.

00:01:12.899 --> 00:01:16.695
A good review asks where data lives before the job starts,

00:01:16.695 --> 00:01:20.835
where outputs go after it finishes, and which files are worth keeping.

00:01:20.835 --> 00:01:23.940
Those questions often reveal avoidable transfers and stale artifacts.
